      "dom_id": "question-45455",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The market value of a sports car, in dollars, can be modeled according to the rule $30\\,000\\cdot (0.85)^{t},$ where $t$ is the number of years since the car was manufactured. What does the base of the exponential expression $(0.85)$ mean in this context?",
      "worked_solution": "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot (1-r)^{t},$ where:\n\n- $a=30\\,000$ represents the initial value of the car (in dollars).\n\n- $1-r=0.85,$ where $r$ is the decay rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time since the start of the observations, in years.\n\nSolving for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n1-r=0.85\\,⟹\\,r=0.15.\n$$\n\nNow, $0.15$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $15%.$ \n\nSo, the base of the exponential expression $0.85$ means that the value of the car depreciates by $15%$ each year.",
