      "dom_id": "question-45713",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The population $C(t)$ of condors in the Andes is given by $C(t)=1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{t}$, where $t$ is the time, in decades, since the last census. How many condors vanished between the second and third decades since the last census?",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the difference in the population of condors between $t=2$ and $t=3$ decades. To calculate this, we find the population after $2$ decades, and we subtract the population after $3$ decades. So we need to evaluate\n\n$$\nC(2)-C(3).\n$$\n\nThe number of condors after $t=2$ decades is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(2) & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.062\\,5 \\\\  & =62\\,500.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe number of condors after $t=3$ decades is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(3) & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot (0.25)^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =15\\,625.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the number of condors lost between the second and third decades is\n\n$$\nC(3)-C(2)=62\\,500-15\\,625=46\\,875.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $46\\,875$ condors vanished between the second and third decades.",
